{"type":"step-attempt","step":"code-review","sessionId":"da43bab3-95bf-4d82-8b68-d1f7011b2501","outcome":{"verdict":"escalation","findingsPath":"specrunner/changes/signal-name-in-interruption/review-feedback-001.md","error":null,"toolResult":{"ok":true,"approved":false,"findings":[{"severity":"medium","resolution":"fixable","file":"src/core/runtime/__tests__/signal-name-in-interruption.test.ts","title":"TC-004 missing: no test pins that exit-guard records omit the `signal` field","rationale":"test-cases.md marks TC-004 as 'must' priority: when exit-guard fires (signal handler did not run), appendInterruption must be called with no `signal` field. Neither the new test file nor the existing exit-guard.test.ts inspects the InterruptionRecord content for field absence. TypeScript compilation confirms the optional field compiles, but runtime pinning is absent. A future refactor that accidentally adds `signal` to exit-guard's call would not be caught."},{"severity":"low","resolution":"fixable","file":"src/core/runtime/local.ts","title":"Misleading exit-code comment: '128 + SIGINT(2)' after handler is shared with SIGTERM/SIGHUP","rationale":"Before this change, signalCleanup was SIGINT-only, so '128 + SIGINT(2)' = 130 was accurate. Now the same handler fires for SIGTERM (128+15=143) and SIGHUP (128+1=129) but still exits with 130. The comment implies the code is signal-specific, contradicting the design decision to keep exit code fixed. Should be updated to something like '// fixed; per-signal exit codes (128+n) are out of scope'.","line":1718},{"severity":"low","resolution":"decision-needed","file":"tests/unit/cli/specrunner-resume-dispatch.test.ts","title":"Incidental out-of-scope change: env-var preservation for DEBUG/SPECRUNNER_LOG_LEVEL","rationale":"The added beforeEach/afterEach logic that saves and restores DEBUG and SPECRUNNER_LOG_LEVEL is unrelated to the signal-name-in-interruption feature. It is a positive test-hygiene improvement (prevents env-var leakage between tests) but falls outside this PR's scope.","options":[{"label":"Keep as-is","consequence":"The test hygiene fix lands in this PR. Minimal risk; tests pass; positive improvement."},{"label":"Revert and move to a separate commit/PR","consequence":"PR scope stays strictly limited to signal-name changes. Requires a separate PR or follow-up commit for the env-var fix."}]}],"evidence":{"checked":19,"skipped":0,"unverified":0},"observations":[{"severity":"low","file":"src/core/runtime/local.ts","title":"Type cast `signal as 'SIGINT' | 'SIGTERM' | 'SIGHUP'` is technically unsound but safe in practice","rationale":"NodeJS.Signals is a broad union; the cast narrows it to the three registered signals. Since only these three are registered via process.on, the cast will never receive an unexpected value at runtime. A type guard would be more strictly correct but is unnecessary given the bounded registration.","line":1698},{"severity":"low","file":"src/core/runtime/__tests__/signal-name-in-interruption.test.ts","title":"process.setMaxListeners(50) is appropriate for multi-runtime signal-handler tests","rationale":"Multiple runtime instances registered in the same test process accumulate SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP handlers. Raising the limit suppresses MaxListenersExceededWarning without impacting test correctness.","line":25},{"severity":"low","file":"src/core/runtime/__tests__/signal-handler-order.test.ts","title":"Existing test calls signalCleanup() without argument — safe due to mocking","rationale":"The existing test casts signalCleanup as '() => Promise<void>' and calls it without argument, so signal=undefined at runtime. JSON serialization omits undefined, and both appendInterruption and persist are mocked. All existing assertions continue to pass unchanged, consistent with design.md Risks section.","line":84}]},"followUpAttempts":0,"transientRetryAttempts":0,"addedTurns":{"reportRetry":0,"postWork":0,"outputRepair":0}},"startedAt":"2026-08-20T15:30:36.559Z","endedAt":"2026-08-20T15:36:31.262Z","commitOid":"021a0192133a7a63cdfb2328fae4bb9b5e51d8b7"}
